## Can someone independent review my SEO agency's work before we renew the contract?

Yes. That is what this review is for. I look at your site as it stands today and compare it with what your agency has reported over the contract. You get a plain answer on three things: what was delivered, whether it moved your search performance, and what to ask before you sign again.

The review runs on the same £495 fixed fee as my technical SEO audit. There is no separate fee for reading the agency reports. The site audit covers technical health, content and AI-search visibility. The agency part covers what was promised, what was reported and what the evidence shows.

## What do I check, and what evidence do I use?

Every finding links back to something you can see yourself. I do not rely on the agency's dashboard or on my opinion of their style.

| What I check | Evidence I use | What it tells you |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Work delivered against invoiced scope** | Your contract or proposal, monthly reports, the live site | Whether the work you paid for exists on the site |
| **Rankings and clicks trend** | Google Search Console, read-only | Whether search performance moved over the contract and on which pages |
| **Technical health** | A crawl of the site, indexation reports | Whether basic problems are still open after months of work |
| **Content shipped** | Published pages, dates, the content plan | Whether new pages target real searches and earn impressions |
| **Links built** | Link lists in reports, a third-party backlink index | Whether links come from relevant sites or from low-quality networks |
| **Reporting honesty** | Report metrics compared with GA4 and Search Console | Whether reports focus on enquiries and revenue or on vanity numbers |
| **AI visibility** | Checks in ChatGPT, Bing Copilot, Google AI Overviews and Perplexity | Whether your brand appears for your key buyer questions on the day I test |

AI answers vary from one run to the next. That part of the review records what I saw on the day. Treat it as a baseline for later comparison.

## We can't judge whether our SEO agency does good work. What are the signs it is worth a second opinion?

Most people who ask this are not SEO specialists. They should not need to be. These signs suggest an independent check is worth £495 before you commit to another year:

- Reports show rankings and traffic but never enquiries, sales or leads
- You cannot name a specific page or fix the agency delivered last quarter
- Organic clicks in Search Console are flat or falling while reports say things are going well
- Link building is reported as a number with no list of the sites
- The same technical issues appear in report after report
- The contract renews automatically and the scope has not changed in a year
- A new marketing lead has inherited the agency and wants a baseline

None of these proves the agency is doing poor work. They are reasons to check. My guide on [how to tell if your SEO agency is lying to you](/blog/seo-agency-lying/) covers the warning signs in more detail.

